WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:06.416 --> 00:10.536 align:middle
Transcript of Chairman Warsh's
Press Conference September 16,

00:10.726 --> 00:13.276 align:middle
2026 CHAIRMAN WARSH.

00:13.456 --> 00:17.586 align:middle
Good day. In the meeting just
concluded, the FOMC decided

00:17.586 --> 00:20.266 align:middle
to raise the target range
for the federal funds rate

00:20.696 --> 00:24.946 align:middle
by a ¼ percentage point
to 3  to 4 percent,

00:25.546 --> 00:28.096 align:middle
in support of the Federal
Reserve's dual mandate.

00:28.466 --> 00:31.046 align:middle
The Committee is
continuing its policy

00:31.046 --> 00:33.616 align:middle
of maintaining ample reserves
in the banking system.

00:34.766 --> 00:36.586 align:middle
As noted in the policy
statement,

00:36.586 --> 00:38.156 align:middle
released just a short while ago,

00:38.806 --> 00:42.036 align:middle
economic activity is
expanding at a solid pace.

00:42.816 --> 00:46.286 align:middle
While uncertainty remains
elevated-owing, in part,

00:46.286 --> 00:49.606 align:middle
to geopolitical
developments-domestic spending

00:49.776 --> 00:50.676 align:middle
has been resilient.

00:51.676 --> 00:56.406 align:middle
Productivity growth-strong; and
capital investment is robust.

00:57.486 --> 00:59.956 align:middle
Job gains have kept
pace with the workforce,

01:00.486 --> 01:02.806 align:middle
and the unemployment
rate has changed little.

01:03.986 --> 01:06.256 align:middle
But inflation remains elevated.

01:07.246 --> 01:10.866 align:middle
Today's policy action will
support a timelier return

01:11.186 --> 01:12.666 align:middle
to the Committee's
2 percent goal.

01:13.306 --> 01:16.106 align:middle
This Committee will
deliver price stability.

01:16.946 --> 01:19.576 align:middle
Now I'll get into
some further detail.

01:20.886 --> 01:22.706 align:middle
Our decision comes at a time

01:22.706 --> 01:25.586 align:middle
when the American economy
appears to be strengthening.

01:26.346 --> 01:28.966 align:middle
New hiring, private-sector
earnings,

01:29.516 --> 01:33.676 align:middle
business capital investment-each
of these markers has improved

01:33.676 --> 01:36.846 align:middle
in recent months and is
pointing in a good direction.

01:37.996 --> 01:41.686 align:middle
Credit flows have been robust,
particularly for businesses.

01:42.506 --> 01:45.656 align:middle
And, as I said at the policy
symposium in Jackson Hole,

01:46.326 --> 01:47.636 align:middle
I would be hard pressed

01:47.636 --> 01:50.566 align:middle
to describe broad financial
conditions as restrictive.

01:51.386 --> 01:55.056 align:middle
This view was widely
shared by the Committee.

01:55.926 --> 01:58.336 align:middle
So we removed a dose
of accommodation.

01:59.556 --> 02:02.786 align:middle
Consider the geopolitical
landscape of shocks

02:02.786 --> 02:05.156 align:middle
and uncertainty, and you begin

02:05.156 --> 02:08.426 align:middle
to appreciate the resilience
of the U.S. economy.

02:09.546 --> 02:12.136 align:middle
Given that resilience,
and the potential

02:12.136 --> 02:14.246 align:middle
for even greater performance,

02:14.726 --> 02:18.946 align:middle
an attitude of optimism is
exactly what I heard inside the

02:18.946 --> 02:20.596 align:middle
FOMC these last two days.

02:21.726 --> 02:24.336 align:middle
One basic sign of
strength is the state

02:24.336 --> 02:25.926 align:middle
of America's labor markets.

02:26.796 --> 02:29.776 align:middle
The jobless-the jobless
rate remains low

02:29.776 --> 02:33.566 align:middle
at around 4.1 percent,
and both job openings

02:33.686 --> 02:35.756 align:middle
and weekly hours
have been increasing.

02:36.836 --> 02:40.486 align:middle
Unemployment claims, on a
four-week moving average,

02:41.016 --> 02:43.316 align:middle
are running at levels
consistent with full employment.

02:44.146 --> 02:47.456 align:middle
So the labor side of the
Fed's congressional remit is

02:47.456 --> 02:48.156 align:middle
in good shape.

02:49.426 --> 02:51.186 align:middle
Yet, for more than five years,

02:51.456 --> 02:53.666 align:middle
inflation has been
running above target.

02:54.786 --> 02:56.636 align:middle
So our predominant focus is

02:56.636 --> 02:59.256 align:middle
on the price-stability
side of our mandate.

03:00.116 --> 03:03.786 align:middle
The plain fact is that
inflation is too high

03:04.236 --> 03:05.706 align:middle
and has been for too long.

03:07.806 --> 03:10.746 align:middle
This summer's inflation
readings do not tell me

03:10.786 --> 03:13.696 align:middle
that underlying trends
have meaningfully improved.

03:14.656 --> 03:19.266 align:middle
Based on the most recent CPI and
PPI data, the 12-month change

03:19.266 --> 03:24.486 align:middle
in total PCE prices likely was
around 3.6 percent in August.

03:25.196 --> 03:29.516 align:middle
Core PCE and CPI prices
are running at about 3.2

03:29.856 --> 03:31.776 align:middle
and 2.4 percent, respectively.

03:32.716 --> 03:36.236 align:middle
Too many categories are
still posting increases

03:36.236 --> 03:40.206 align:middle
above 3 percent on both
a 6- and 12-month basis.

03:41.356 --> 03:42.936 align:middle
I noted in Jackson Hole

03:43.356 --> 03:46.366 align:middle
that overall commodity
prices also bear watching.

03:47.306 --> 03:50.166 align:middle
And, over the intermeeting
period, the prices of many

03:50.166 --> 03:52.016 align:middle
of these key inputs have risen.

03:53.656 --> 03:57.926 align:middle
Since my first FOMC meeting as
Chairman in June, my colleagues

03:57.926 --> 04:00.586 align:middle
and I have been unequivocal
in our commitment

04:00.586 --> 04:06.026 align:middle
to price stability and to our 2
percent PCE inflation objective.

04:06.826 --> 04:09.166 align:middle
At our July meeting,
we all agreed

04:09.166 --> 04:10.686 align:middle
that inflation remained
too high,

04:11.466 --> 04:14.046 align:middle
and we expressed our
joint readiness to act

04:14.816 --> 04:16.456 align:middle
as circumstances might require.

04:17.246 --> 04:19.376 align:middle
And a good majority
of my colleagues

04:19.596 --> 04:22.376 align:middle
and I thought the wiser
course then would be

04:22.376 --> 04:25.216 align:middle
to await new information
in the intermeeting period.

04:26.146 --> 04:29.976 align:middle
Last month in Wyoming, I
expressed my commitment

04:29.976 --> 04:33.656 align:middle
to a monetary policy
discipline, not to a decision.

04:34.606 --> 04:36.226 align:middle
I defined the standard
for action.

04:37.116 --> 04:41.216 align:middle
We must be confident that
underlying inflation is moving

04:41.216 --> 04:44.746 align:middle
to our objective, clearly
and at sufficient speed.

04:46.176 --> 04:51.756 align:middle
Today the FOMC decided that this
standard has not been satisfied.

04:53.046 --> 04:55.736 align:middle
The Committee's unanimous
vote shows our resolve

04:55.736 --> 04:58.626 align:middle
to achieve price stability
on a timelier basis.

04:59.616 --> 05:01.416 align:middle
We aim to ensure that credit

05:01.416 --> 05:04.636 align:middle
and financial conditions
are consistent over time

05:04.906 --> 05:09.616 align:middle
with our mandate, that relative
price changes in some sectors

05:09.616 --> 05:14.546 align:middle
of the economy do not broaden,
that inflation compensation

05:14.546 --> 05:16.216 align:middle
in market prices stays low,

05:16.996 --> 05:19.966 align:middle
and that inflation expectations
remain well anchored.

05:21.296 --> 05:23.556 align:middle
This afternoon, you also
received the Summary

05:23.556 --> 05:25.036 align:middle
of Economic Projections.

05:25.776 --> 05:28.196 align:middle
It reflects the views of my
colleagues on the Committee,

05:28.676 --> 05:31.586 align:middle
but, as in June, I have not
offered a projection of my own.

05:32.406 --> 05:35.446 align:middle
But, like in June, I said I
would faithfully discharge the

05:35.446 --> 05:37.506 align:middle
summary of their
projections, so here goes.

05:38.376 --> 05:42.026 align:middle
In the summary's median
projections, real GDP rises

05:42.026 --> 05:45.896 align:middle
at 2.3 percent this year,
2.4 percent next year.

05:46.686 --> 05:51.346 align:middle
Total PCE inflation runs at
3.7 percent this year and falls

05:51.346 --> 05:53.006 align:middle
to 2.3 percent next year.

05:53.726 --> 05:57.376 align:middle
The unemployment rate holds
steady at about 4.1 percent.

05:58.526 --> 06:00.336 align:middle
The median participant judges

06:00.336 --> 06:04.106 align:middle
that the appropriate federal
funds rate to be 4.1 percent

06:04.686 --> 06:07.796 align:middle
at the end of this year and
to remain there next year.

06:08.746 --> 06:10.196 align:middle
Inflation risks are

06:10.196 --> 06:13.906 align:middle
to the upside while labor
risks are roughly balanced.

06:15.306 --> 06:19.006 align:middle
In my meetings these last
few weeks-in Jackson Hole;

06:19.556 --> 06:22.826 align:middle
in Asheville at the G-20
meeting, which the U.S. hosted;

06:23.476 --> 06:27.126 align:middle
and at a central bank conference
in Basel-it was evident

06:27.176 --> 06:31.386 align:middle
that most advanced economies
are facing price pressures.

06:31.906 --> 06:34.836 align:middle
Their central banks are
making their own judgments,

06:35.046 --> 06:36.876 align:middle
consistent with their
own remits.

06:37.666 --> 06:40.316 align:middle
Our decision today
reflects our best judgment

06:40.586 --> 06:41.866 align:middle
in service to our remit.

06:43.856 --> 06:48.026 align:middle
The Fed has a role in sustaining
the economic progress happening

06:48.026 --> 06:51.556 align:middle
in America right now and
the rising opportunities

06:51.556 --> 06:52.236 align:middle
that come with it.

06:53.516 --> 06:57.066 align:middle
Those who are least well
off have the most to gain

06:57.616 --> 06:59.046 align:middle
from a durable expansion,

06:59.436 --> 07:02.506 align:middle
a solid labor market,
and stable prices.

07:03.536 --> 07:06.836 align:middle
We at the Fed are
unwavering in our vital

07:06.836 --> 07:09.566 align:middle
and straightforward
purpose: full employment

07:09.666 --> 07:13.356 align:middle
and price stability, and a
thriving American economy

07:13.706 --> 07:15.146 align:middle
that sets the standard
for the world.

07:15.876 --> 07:17.616 align:middle
And, with that, I'll take
a few of your questions.

07:17.986 --> 07:19.746 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

07:21.786 --> 07:24.206 align:middle
Richard. RICHARD ESCOBEDO.

07:24.206 --> 07:24.676 align:middle
Thank you.

07:24.676 --> 07:27.286 align:middle
Chair Warsh, thank
you for doing this.

07:27.766 --> 07:30.266 align:middle
I'm Richard Escobedo with CBS.

07:30.396 --> 07:32.386 align:middle
Let me navigate over
to my questions.

07:32.566 --> 07:35.096 align:middle
You know, a ¼ point rate hike
does not reopen the Strait

07:35.096 --> 07:35.786 align:middle
of Hormuz.

07:35.896 --> 07:39.476 align:middle
And so I wonder how you think
these smaller rate hikes will be

07:39.476 --> 07:42.506 align:middle
effective when it can't
necessarily address the energy

07:42.506 --> 07:44.496 align:middle
supply side of inflationary
pressures.

07:44.596 --> 07:45.096 align:middle
CHAIRMAN WARSH.

07:45.096 --> 07:47.396 align:middle
It's a-it's a-it's a
good question, Richard.

07:47.876 --> 07:52.406 align:middle
We cannot affect any individual
price, whether it be oil prices,

07:52.406 --> 07:54.566 align:middle
whether it be foodstuffs
at the grocery store.

07:54.626 --> 07:59.266 align:middle
But what we can do, and will
do, is ensure that any change

07:59.266 --> 08:03.026 align:middle
in relative prices don't
broaden out, don't have second-

08:03.026 --> 08:04.556 align:middle
and third-order effects
in the economy.

08:04.556 --> 08:06.836 align:middle
That's what we're tasked to
do, and that's what we will do.

08:07.156 --> 08:08.686 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

08:09.076 --> 08:12.086 align:middle
Colby. COLBY SMITH.

08:12.826 --> 08:13.316 align:middle
Thank you.

08:13.316 --> 08:14.576 align:middle
Colby Smith from
the New York Times.

08:15.066 --> 08:17.596 align:middle
When the Fed starts raising
rates, it generally follows

08:17.596 --> 08:18.646 align:middle
with a sequence of hikes.

08:18.976 --> 08:21.856 align:middle
Is there anything different
in today's assessment

08:22.266 --> 08:24.376 align:middle
of the economic conditions
that would suggest

08:24.376 --> 08:28.336 align:middle
that the typical pattern does
not apply, and I guess second

08:28.336 --> 08:30.956 align:middle
to that, what impact do you
expect higher rates to have

08:30.956 --> 08:32.586 align:middle
at this juncture if the bulk

08:32.586 --> 08:34.426 align:middle
of what is keeping
inflation elevated is stemming

08:34.426 --> 08:35.236 align:middle
from supply shocks?

08:35.266 --> 08:35.566 align:middle
CHAIRMAN WARSH.

08:35.566 --> 08:37.626 align:middle
So we're trying to get to
more people, so now I get

08:37.626 --> 08:40.336 align:middle
to cherry-pick my preferred
question from you, Colby.

08:41.236 --> 08:42.586 align:middle
This won't surprise you: I'm not

08:42.586 --> 08:44.136 align:middle
in the forward guidance
business.

08:44.286 --> 08:47.306 align:middle
The decision we made today
was a sober decision,

08:47.686 --> 08:50.296 align:middle
serious decision,
responsible decision,

08:50.816 --> 08:53.296 align:middle
one that we have been
preparing for and thinking

08:53.296 --> 08:55.846 align:middle
about in my 110 or 20 days here.

08:56.556 --> 08:59.796 align:middle
You heard from other people
in the dots effectively,

08:59.946 --> 09:01.216 align:middle
what their forecasts are.

09:01.656 --> 09:05.156 align:middle
I'm not going to prejudge
any future decisions we make.

09:05.156 --> 09:08.146 align:middle
You might have heard
me say in Jackson Hole,

09:08.146 --> 09:10.956 align:middle
I committed to a discipline,
a set of principles.

09:11.356 --> 09:13.016 align:middle
I committed to look
outside the window

09:13.016 --> 09:15.036 align:middle
and see what I can observe.

09:16.076 --> 09:17.226 align:middle
That's what I did in Jackson.

09:17.226 --> 09:17.966 align:middle
That's what we did today.

09:19.596 --> 09:19.866 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

09:19.866 --> 09:20.266 align:middle
Edward Lawrence.

09:20.296 --> 09:20.916 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

09:22.476 --> 09:23.116 align:middle
Thank you, Mr. Chairman.

09:23.116 --> 09:24.576 align:middle
Edward Lawrence from
Fox Business.

09:24.766 --> 09:27.706 align:middle
So the market priced
in a 90 percent chance

09:27.706 --> 09:28.676 align:middle
of a rate hike today.

09:29.086 --> 09:31.696 align:middle
You don't want the Fed
to lead the markets.

09:31.696 --> 09:34.106 align:middle
Was this a market-led rate hike?

09:34.546 --> 09:36.456 align:middle
And then, with that, the
bond yields are going up.

09:36.456 --> 09:37.566 align:middle
That's one of the indicators.

09:38.036 --> 09:39.746 align:middle
Is debt part of that issue?

09:40.546 --> 09:41.486 align:middle
CHAIRMAN WARSH.

09:41.566 --> 09:43.776 align:middle
So I've said this
before-I'll repeat it:

09:43.966 --> 09:46.916 align:middle
The Fed has an enormous
amount of power.

09:47.686 --> 09:49.476 align:middle
These are decisions we make.

09:50.256 --> 09:54.156 align:middle
But getting the understanding
right between financial markets

09:54.156 --> 09:55.536 align:middle
and the Fed is a balance

09:55.586 --> 09:57.496 align:middle
that I've long thought
could be better struck.

09:57.976 --> 10:00.806 align:middle
We made this decision today
based on our assessment

10:00.806 --> 10:05.466 align:middle
of the situation, based on our
assessment of the trajectory

10:05.466 --> 10:08.046 align:middle
for employment, based
on our judgment

10:08.046 --> 10:10.556 align:middle
on the strength of the economy.

10:10.696 --> 10:14.746 align:middle
Sometimes the market tries
to prejudge our outcomes.

10:14.946 --> 10:19.206 align:middle
I'll observe market prices and,
and see what they have to say.

10:19.206 --> 10:20.296 align:middle
But today was our decision.

10:20.296 --> 10:21.896 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

10:21.896 --> 10:25.156 align:middle
Steve Thompson, with
the Washington Post.

10:28.976 --> 10:30.286 align:middle
[Inaudible] MICHELLE SMITH.

10:30.436 --> 10:31.366 align:middle
Elizabeth Schulze.

10:32.876 --> 10:34.276 align:middle
ELIZABETH SCHULZE.

10:34.556 --> 10:35.026 align:middle
Thanks so much.

10:35.026 --> 10:36.596 align:middle
Elizabeth Schulze with ABC News.

10:37.026 --> 10:37.716 align:middle
Big picture, I wonder

10:37.716 --> 10:40.226 align:middle
if you could tell us just what
will this move today actually do

10:40.226 --> 10:41.346 align:middle
for American consumers?

10:41.386 --> 10:45.126 align:middle
And I have to ask, what is your
message here to President Trump,

10:45.126 --> 10:48.056 align:middle
who has repeatedly called to cut
interest rates, not raise them?

10:48.056 --> 10:49.496 align:middle
CHAIRMAN WARSH.

10:50.106 --> 10:51.376 align:middle
I've got nothing for you

10:51.376 --> 10:53.996 align:middle
on the-on the discussion
with the President.

10:54.186 --> 10:56.236 align:middle
So-but I won't make that
count as your question.

10:56.626 --> 11:00.806 align:middle
On the American people, as I
said in my prepared remarks,

11:00.916 --> 11:04.096 align:middle
the least well off are
the ones who have the most

11:04.096 --> 11:05.776 align:middle
to gain from stable prices.

11:06.176 --> 11:10.466 align:middle
The decision we made today was
the right decision to deliver

11:10.466 --> 11:13.556 align:middle
on the remit that Congress gave
us to ensure stable prices.

11:14.086 --> 11:17.726 align:middle
Moreover, I would say, because
of the underlying strength

11:17.726 --> 11:20.706 align:middle
of the economy, because
we are, as I mentioned,

11:21.136 --> 11:24.096 align:middle
largely acting consistent
with full employment,

11:24.346 --> 11:26.246 align:middle
we can be focused
on stable prices.

11:26.576 --> 11:29.416 align:middle
Some months ago, I said we
will deliver stable prices.

11:29.646 --> 11:31.196 align:middle
Today's action is
consistent with that.

11:31.856 --> 11:33.076 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

11:33.136 --> 11:33.976 align:middle
Chris Rugaber.

11:34.686 --> 11:36.836 align:middle
CHRIS RUGABER.

11:37.376 --> 11:38.276 align:middle
Hi. How are you?

11:38.276 --> 11:39.046 align:middle
Thank you.

11:39.046 --> 11:41.926 align:middle
Can you tell us a little
more about what changed

11:41.926 --> 11:46.036 align:middle
from the July meeting when, as
you noted, the Fed did stand pat

11:46.506 --> 11:50.326 align:middle
until today, and as part of
that, could you give us a sense

11:50.326 --> 11:51.446 align:middle
of whether or not things

11:51.446 --> 11:54.296 align:middle
like the retail sales
report today suggested

11:54.296 --> 11:55.626 align:middle
that demand is heating up and,

11:55.626 --> 11:58.506 align:middle
and possibly threatening
higher prices as well?

11:58.606 --> 11:58.816 align:middle
Thank you.

11:58.876 --> 11:59.256 align:middle
CHAIRMAN WARSH.

11:59.436 --> 12:03.096 align:middle
Yeah. So, as you might know, I'm
not a data-point-dependent guy.

12:03.746 --> 12:07.236 align:middle
So I won't-I won't react one
way or another to data that,

12:07.316 --> 12:08.926 align:middle
that shows up on our doorstep.

12:09.606 --> 12:12.086 align:middle
But, on your first question-I
think the more important

12:12.086 --> 12:15.586 align:middle
one-what transpired in the
seven weeks since we last met?

12:15.706 --> 12:17.616 align:middle
First, I would say
a good majority

12:17.616 --> 12:20.796 align:middle
of my colleagues seven weeks
ago thought seven weeks is a

12:20.796 --> 12:21.606 align:middle
good investment.

12:21.706 --> 12:24.576 align:middle
It's a way to buy time so we
could make a wise decision.

12:24.956 --> 12:26.916 align:middle
I'll highlight three
things that have happened

12:26.916 --> 12:28.146 align:middle
in that intermeeting period.

12:28.746 --> 12:32.366 align:middle
One is, I made out a
judgment seven weeks ago

12:32.366 --> 12:33.796 align:middle
about the strength
of the economy.

12:34.386 --> 12:37.026 align:middle
There's been a pretty
wide-ranging set of data,

12:37.026 --> 12:38.386 align:middle
including in the labor markets,

12:38.456 --> 12:39.996 align:middle
that the economy
has strengthened.

12:40.476 --> 12:42.876 align:middle
You might have heard me say that
in Jackson Hole a few weeks ago.

12:43.416 --> 12:46.256 align:middle
It's a judgment that I
have, and the Committee has.

12:47.066 --> 12:52.266 align:middle
Second, inflation trends-I said
in Jackson Hole, trends matter.

12:52.906 --> 12:55.756 align:middle
I said in Jackson Hole, we
need to look outside the window

12:55.756 --> 12:57.156 align:middle
and interrogate reality.

12:58.536 --> 13:00.386 align:middle
My judgment some weeks ago was,

13:00.386 --> 13:04.026 align:middle
the inflation summer trends
weren't passing the test.

13:04.566 --> 13:06.136 align:middle
I've seen very little
information

13:06.136 --> 13:08.376 align:middle
since that would make me
reverse that decision,

13:08.376 --> 13:09.156 align:middle
so I've stuck with it.

13:09.156 --> 13:11.256 align:middle
And the third thing
that's changed

13:11.256 --> 13:13.186 align:middle
in seven weeks is geopolitics.

13:13.186 --> 13:16.476 align:middle
And there's no hiding from
hot spots around the world,

13:16.966 --> 13:20.416 align:middle
and our judgment about
what is the most likely,

13:20.416 --> 13:21.416 align:middle
or least likely,

13:21.416 --> 13:23.396 align:middle
of the geopolitical
situation has changed.

13:23.696 --> 13:27.646 align:middle
All three of those things
led-lend themselves to a firm,

13:27.646 --> 13:28.796 align:middle
unanimous decision today.

13:30.106 --> 13:30.446 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

13:30.446 --> 13:33.166 align:middle
Claire. CLAIRE JONES.

13:34.526 --> 13:36.726 align:middle
Claire Jones, Financial Times.

13:37.826 --> 13:41.076 align:middle
You said today's decision
removes a dose of accommodation

13:41.456 --> 13:44.566 align:middle
in your view, and perhaps
if you could share views

13:44.566 --> 13:45.686 align:middle
around the table, too.

13:46.566 --> 13:50.106 align:middle
Are interest rates now at a
level that you would describe

13:50.176 --> 13:51.566 align:middle
as restrictive or not?

13:52.106 --> 13:52.396 align:middle
Thank you.

13:53.096 --> 13:53.756 align:middle
CHAIRMAN WARSH.

13:55.226 --> 13:57.826 align:middle
I've described previously
I found it difficult

13:57.856 --> 14:00.326 align:middle
to describe financial
conditions as restrictive.

14:00.326 --> 14:01.946 align:middle
I think I said I
was hard pressed.

14:02.706 --> 14:06.186 align:middle
What I heard around the table
in the last couple of days is,

14:06.276 --> 14:09.366 align:middle
my colleagues were hard pressed
to describe it that way, too.

14:11.016 --> 14:15.696 align:middle
We removed a dose of
accommodation so that financial

14:15.696 --> 14:17.976 align:middle
and credit conditions
would be more consistent

14:17.976 --> 14:19.466 align:middle
with our ultimate objectives.

14:19.986 --> 14:20.916 align:middle
That was the decision.

14:20.916 --> 14:23.636 align:middle
That was our judgment,
and we'll continue

14:23.636 --> 14:25.036 align:middle
to evaluate that prospectively.

14:26.526 --> 14:27.226 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

14:27.316 --> 14:29.736 align:middle
Steve Liesman.

14:30.336 --> 14:30.786 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

14:30.786 --> 14:31.986 align:middle
Thank you, Mr. Chairman.

14:31.986 --> 14:32.776 align:middle
Steve Liesman, CNBC.

14:32.776 --> 14:34.306 align:middle
I want to follow up
on that question.

14:34.926 --> 14:39.186 align:middle
Previously, most Fed officials
have described the rate

14:39.186 --> 14:40.666 align:middle
as modestly restrictive.

14:41.406 --> 14:42.836 align:middle
And if you removed
accommodation,

14:43.056 --> 14:44.336 align:middle
could you give us your sense

14:44.336 --> 14:48.276 align:middle
of where the fed funds
rate is relative to neutral

14:48.276 --> 14:51.646 align:middle
and some detail, if you
wouldn't mind, on your sense of,

14:52.056 --> 14:54.316 align:middle
is there a short-term
neutral rate you're aiming for

14:54.316 --> 14:55.456 align:middle
and a longer-term neutral rate?

14:55.456 --> 14:57.526 align:middle
Do you think in terms of those?

14:57.676 --> 14:58.246 align:middle
CHAIRMAN WARSH.

14:58.246 --> 14:59.456 align:middle
In a word, no.

14:59.606 --> 15:01.516 align:middle
In a few words, I'd say this.

15:02.046 --> 15:06.306 align:middle
I've always been
interested in a neutral rate

15:06.306 --> 15:07.466 align:middle
as an academic matter.

15:07.976 --> 15:10.166 align:middle
Back when I learned economics,
we used to think of it

15:10.166 --> 15:11.606 align:middle
as a Wicksellian rate.

15:11.946 --> 15:13.686 align:middle
It's a real equilibrium rate.

15:14.196 --> 15:16.526 align:middle
It's useful academically.

15:16.526 --> 15:19.956 align:middle
It's, it's a discussion to
help us think about policy.

15:20.306 --> 15:23.206 align:middle
Do I think it has any
operational effect

15:23.206 --> 15:25.086 align:middle
on decisions that we make today?

15:25.206 --> 15:25.736 align:middle
No, I don't.

15:26.906 --> 15:28.276 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

15:29.386 --> 15:32.416 align:middle
Victoria. VICTORIA GUIDA.

15:32.416 --> 15:33.886 align:middle
Hi, Victoria Guida
with Politico.

15:33.966 --> 15:36.656 align:middle
I wanted to ask, you've
talked about how you don't

15:36.656 --> 15:38.516 align:middle
like data dependence,
including today.

15:38.516 --> 15:41.826 align:middle
But heading into this
meeting, there was a ton

15:41.826 --> 15:43.226 align:middle
of focus on the August CPI.

15:43.226 --> 15:47.126 align:middle
And I'm wondering if you think
that that was appropriate

15:47.126 --> 15:49.666 align:middle
on the part of markets,
or if you learned anything

15:49.666 --> 15:52.316 align:middle
about how you might approach
communications going forward?

15:52.876 --> 15:53.726 align:middle
CHAIRMAN WARSH.

15:53.856 --> 15:58.406 align:middle
So market participants and
reporters, I think generally

15:58.406 --> 16:00.756 align:middle
over the course of
the last decade or so,

16:01.456 --> 16:05.966 align:middle
have grown accustomed to
waiting somewhat breathlessly

16:05.966 --> 16:07.796 align:middle
on a data point.

16:07.936 --> 16:08.906 align:middle
That isn't my view.

16:08.906 --> 16:14.466 align:middle
I was not waiting breathlessly
on what any particular data was,

16:14.466 --> 16:16.196 align:middle
whether it was retail
sales this morning

16:16.196 --> 16:18.216 align:middle
or a CPI "print" last week.

16:18.926 --> 16:21.466 align:middle
I'll just reiterate:
Trends matter.

16:22.326 --> 16:23.736 align:middle
Data points are noisy.

16:24.306 --> 16:28.346 align:middle
Data point dependence is
a dangerous preoccupation.

16:28.606 --> 16:31.236 align:middle
It's not something
that concerns me.

16:31.466 --> 16:32.846 align:middle
Markets over time will come

16:32.846 --> 16:37.196 align:middle
to understand how this Fed makes
its decisions, what's relevant

16:37.196 --> 16:39.266 align:middle
and not, and I wouldn't
want to editorialize

16:39.266 --> 16:40.176 align:middle
that for them beyond it.

16:40.316 --> 16:40.806 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

16:41.306 --> 16:42.036 align:middle
Zach Halaschak.

16:42.296 --> 16:42.926 align:middle
ZACHARY HALASCHAK.

16:43.586 --> 16:45.376 align:middle
Thanks for doing
this, Mr. Chairman.

16:45.376 --> 16:46.806 align:middle
Zach Halaschak, the
Washington Examiner.

16:47.126 --> 16:48.086 align:middle
I'm just curious.

16:48.086 --> 16:49.976 align:middle
A couple weeks ago
the President sent

16:49.976 --> 16:52.106 align:middle
out a message essentially
threatening to cut off trade

16:52.106 --> 16:54.016 align:middle
to certain countries
unless rates were lowered.

16:54.296 --> 16:56.686 align:middle
Obviously a unanimous
decision to do the opposite.

16:56.926 --> 16:58.436 align:middle
What would you say
to investors who sort

16:58.436 --> 17:00.936 align:middle
of believe this is another
test of the Fed's independence?

17:01.196 --> 17:03.376 align:middle
And then, kind of, when
was the last time you spoke

17:03.376 --> 17:03.946 align:middle
with the President?

17:03.946 --> 17:06.326 align:middle
Do you anticipate a
post-decision meeting,

17:06.326 --> 17:07.846 align:middle
or- CHAIRMAN WARSH.

17:07.846 --> 17:10.406 align:middle
You gave me a long menu
from which to choose.

17:10.446 --> 17:11.596 align:middle
They're all very tempting.

17:11.596 --> 17:14.526 align:middle
I don't have anything
for you on,

17:14.526 --> 17:16.276 align:middle
on discussions with
the President.

17:16.276 --> 17:19.506 align:middle
And I'm not a Wall
Street newsletter.

17:19.806 --> 17:22.426 align:middle
Part of the independence

17:22.426 --> 17:25.056 align:middle
of the Federal Reserve
is we stay in our lane.

17:25.936 --> 17:27.656 align:middle
Independence is a
two-way street.

17:27.926 --> 17:29.636 align:middle
We'll let people
that do trade policy

17:29.636 --> 17:31.716 align:middle
and fiscal policy stay
in their lane, too.

17:32.106 --> 17:33.656 align:middle
That's how we can stand up here

17:33.656 --> 17:34.726 align:middle
and call them the
way we see them.

17:36.226 --> 17:36.846 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

17:37.026 --> 17:38.286 align:middle
Brian Cheung.

17:39.076 --> 17:40.006 align:middle
BRIAN CHEUNG.

17:40.346 --> 17:41.676 align:middle
Hi there. Brian Cheung
with NBC News.

17:41.676 --> 17:43.246 align:middle
Just wondering if
you could explain

17:43.246 --> 17:46.126 align:middle
who is the least well off,
and what does a rate hike do

17:46.126 --> 17:47.506 align:middle
when those people
might be pinched

17:47.506 --> 17:50.676 align:middle
by higher mortgage rates, higher
gas, higher grocery prices,

17:50.676 --> 17:52.036 align:middle
and now broad higher rates?

17:52.276 --> 17:53.456 align:middle
CHAIRMAN WARSH.

17:53.706 --> 17:55.126 align:middle
Yeah. It's a fair question.

17:55.336 --> 17:58.756 align:middle
In the macroeconomics, we
tend to look at aggregates

17:58.756 --> 18:03.326 align:middle
around here, aggregate GDP,
overall labor market trends,

18:03.536 --> 18:04.786 align:middle
the state of inflation.

18:04.846 --> 18:07.546 align:middle
A lot of people in
Washington spend a lot of time

18:07.546 --> 18:09.036 align:middle
on distributional consequences,

18:09.036 --> 18:10.956 align:middle
and that's their job
and their business.

18:11.556 --> 18:14.646 align:middle
What I was referring to in the
least well off tend to be people

18:14.646 --> 18:18.006 align:middle
that don't own financial
assets-call that a bit less

18:18.036 --> 18:20.246 align:middle
than 50 percent of the country.

18:20.536 --> 18:21.826 align:middle
They don't have equity
in their home.

18:21.826 --> 18:25.436 align:middle
They don't have equity
in a 401k plan.

18:25.436 --> 18:27.786 align:middle
So they're living
off their paycheck

18:27.786 --> 18:29.406 align:middle
that comes every
couple of weeks.

18:29.876 --> 18:31.606 align:middle
The thing that we
can do consistent

18:31.606 --> 18:33.126 align:middle
with our mandate is two things.

18:33.406 --> 18:35.506 align:middle
Ask ourselves, is the
country running more

18:35.506 --> 18:36.826 align:middle
or less at full employment?

18:37.286 --> 18:39.216 align:middle
And we've done that.

18:39.216 --> 18:41.416 align:middle
That doesn't mean that
individuals aren't searching

18:41.416 --> 18:43.546 align:middle
for a job, but in
aggregate, we're running more

18:43.546 --> 18:44.726 align:middle
or less at full employment.

18:44.946 --> 18:48.216 align:middle
If so, we can then look at the
other side of the-our mandate,

18:48.216 --> 18:49.616 align:middle
and let that be our focus.

18:50.086 --> 18:53.006 align:middle
And stable prices,
an environment

18:53.006 --> 18:54.966 align:middle
where inflation is
running consistent

18:54.966 --> 18:58.126 align:middle
with our 2 percent
objective, offers good news.

18:58.126 --> 18:59.916 align:middle
Because that way, when
they get their wages,

18:59.916 --> 19:01.906 align:middle
they can put their
head above water

19:02.206 --> 19:05.046 align:middle
and deliver real
take-home pay increases.

19:05.356 --> 19:07.446 align:middle
We don't have total
responsibility for it.

19:07.446 --> 19:09.006 align:middle
But we do have responsibility

19:09.006 --> 19:12.266 align:middle
for stable prices-as I've
said before, inflation is a,

19:12.266 --> 19:15.236 align:middle
is a choice-and today we
took a step in delivering it.

19:16.346 --> 19:17.396 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

19:17.846 --> 19:19.166 align:middle
Myriam. MYRIAM LEMETAYER.

19:19.166 --> 19:19.816 align:middle
Yes, this is Myriam-[inaudible].

19:20.016 --> 19:20.446 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

19:20.746 --> 19:21.056 align:middle
[inaudible]-the mic.

19:21.686 --> 19:22.296 align:middle
MYRIAM LEMETAYER.

19:22.296 --> 19:22.636 align:middle
Yes. Sorry.

19:22.636 --> 19:24.856 align:middle
Myriam Lemetayer from
Agence France-Presse, AFP.

19:24.856 --> 19:26.666 align:middle
Are you looking at the
other central banks,

19:26.946 --> 19:30.036 align:middle
and what do you think about the
European Central Bank's move?

19:30.396 --> 19:33.456 align:middle
They hiked twice this
year but not in a row.

19:33.676 --> 19:34.096 align:middle
Thank you.

19:34.846 --> 19:35.566 align:middle
CHAIRMAN WARSH.

19:35.566 --> 19:39.656 align:middle
Well, I don't ask them
to prejudge decisions

19:39.656 --> 19:40.596 align:middle
that we're going to make,

19:40.596 --> 19:42.686 align:middle
so I won't prejudge
decisions that they make.

19:43.336 --> 19:45.926 align:middle
But I will say this,
I've spent some time

19:45.926 --> 19:48.306 align:middle
with foreign central
bank counterparts,

19:48.946 --> 19:50.996 align:middle
not just in the last 20
years, but over the course

19:50.996 --> 19:53.886 align:middle
of the last several weeks, as
I mentioned, in Jackson Hole,

19:54.506 --> 19:57.846 align:middle
at the G-20 meeting we
hosted in North Carolina,

19:58.256 --> 20:00.696 align:middle
and at a central bank
meeting in Basel.

20:01.456 --> 20:03.626 align:middle
What I heard around
the table from most

20:03.626 --> 20:06.276 align:middle
of the advanced economies
is they're suffering

20:06.276 --> 20:07.406 align:middle
from price pressures, too.

20:08.306 --> 20:10.706 align:middle
And they're making their
own choices consistent

20:10.706 --> 20:11.426 align:middle
with their remit.

20:11.426 --> 20:13.966 align:middle
It tells me a couple things.

20:13.966 --> 20:17.696 align:middle
One is, when the Federal
Reserve makes a policy choice,

20:18.546 --> 20:21.136 align:middle
it matters not just to the
U.S. economy, but it spills

20:21.136 --> 20:22.156 align:middle
over to the rest of the world.

20:22.686 --> 20:24.586 align:middle
To a lesser extent,
that's true for them, too.

20:25.156 --> 20:27.506 align:middle
When foreign central
banks make decisions,

20:27.506 --> 20:30.846 align:middle
where they're confronted with
higher prices, and they choose,

20:30.846 --> 20:34.596 align:middle
consistent with their
remit, to raise rates,

20:35.046 --> 20:37.816 align:middle
then they're helping to quash
inflation in their countries.

20:37.946 --> 20:40.366 align:middle
And there's spillovers and
spillbacks in both directions.

20:40.576 --> 20:41.756 align:middle
Beyond that, I won't opine

20:41.756 --> 20:43.636 align:middle
on what other central
banks may choose

20:43.636 --> 20:44.756 align:middle
to do this week or beyond.

20:45.766 --> 20:46.116 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

20:46.306 --> 20:46.686 align:middle
Nick Timiraos.

20:48.036 --> 20:49.536 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

20:50.206 --> 20:50.516 align:middle
Thank you.

20:50.516 --> 20:52.046 align:middle
Nick Timiraos of the
Wall Street Journal.

20:52.106 --> 20:54.786 align:middle
Chairman Warsh, last fall
you expressed concern

20:54.856 --> 20:56.746 align:middle
that the Fed was about
to make its, quote,

20:56.856 --> 20:58.546 align:middle
sixth or seventh big mistake,

20:59.046 --> 21:02.396 align:middle
by deciding the economy was too
strong to justify lower rates.

21:02.756 --> 21:04.136 align:middle
Now today you raised rates.

21:04.136 --> 21:08.136 align:middle
Can you give some sense as to
what changed your own assessment

21:08.546 --> 21:10.936 align:middle
of the U.S. economy
between then and now?

21:10.936 --> 21:11.666 align:middle
CHAIRMAN WARSH.

21:11.866 --> 21:14.446 align:middle
So I don't-I don't
remember the full context,

21:14.446 --> 21:17.916 align:middle
but I can tell you, Nick,
about the state of growth now.

21:18.646 --> 21:22.456 align:middle
My suspicion, 110 or 20
days ago when I showed up,

21:22.456 --> 21:25.216 align:middle
was that the U.S.
economy was strengthening.

21:26.396 --> 21:30.536 align:middle
Even over the last several weeks
I think we now have data broadly

21:30.536 --> 21:34.066 align:middle
defined that says the economy
has indeed strengthened.

21:34.696 --> 21:39.176 align:middle
Underlying growth is higher.

21:39.386 --> 21:40.836 align:middle
Inflation is the problem.

21:41.006 --> 21:43.786 align:middle
Stable prices have been
the problem for now more

21:43.786 --> 21:45.006 align:middle
than five and a half years.

21:45.556 --> 21:48.576 align:middle
So what the Committee decided
to do today was take an action

21:48.576 --> 21:52.426 align:middle
to ensure a timelier return to
our price-stability objective.

21:53.246 --> 21:56.246 align:middle
Price stability is
foundational to economic growth,

21:56.246 --> 21:58.896 align:middle
and I think we took an important
step today to deliver it.

21:58.896 --> 22:02.226 align:middle
And we did it in part
by removing the dose

22:02.226 --> 22:04.456 align:middle
of accommodation that
I-that I mentioned before.

22:06.106 --> 22:06.376 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

22:06.376 --> 22:07.116 align:middle
Neil Irwin.

22:08.576 --> 22:08.926 align:middle
NEIL IRWIN.

22:09.076 --> 22:10.756 align:middle
Thank you, Mr. Chairman.

22:11.076 --> 22:12.216 align:middle
Neil Irwin with Axios.

22:12.496 --> 22:14.366 align:middle
Longer-term bond yields
are up quite a bit

22:14.366 --> 22:16.386 align:middle
over the last few months,
especially the last few weeks.

22:16.556 --> 22:18.156 align:middle
What do you believe the
bond market is telling you,

22:18.156 --> 22:20.106 align:middle
especially about the growth
outlook, the neutral rate,

22:20.546 --> 22:22.486 align:middle
and what are the implications
for monetary policy?

22:22.816 --> 22:24.006 align:middle
CHAIRMAN WARSH.

22:24.006 --> 22:26.226 align:middle
Yeah. Let me speak
to the history.

22:26.646 --> 22:29.286 align:middle
What bond market
prices do prospectively,

22:29.356 --> 22:31.216 align:middle
I want to let them do-I want

22:31.216 --> 22:34.516 align:middle
to let them tell me
any story they wish to.

22:34.516 --> 22:36.156 align:middle
I want to try to
interrogate that.

22:36.886 --> 22:38.956 align:middle
But why did yields
rise, let's say,

22:38.956 --> 22:41.696 align:middle
since the last FOMC
meeting until this?

22:42.296 --> 22:44.696 align:middle
I'll give you three,
three reasons,

22:44.696 --> 22:46.936 align:middle
but I would say these things
tend to be overdetermined.

22:46.936 --> 22:48.766 align:middle
This is a complicated
set of things

22:49.246 --> 22:52.006 align:middle
that are affecting the most
important asset anywhere

22:52.006 --> 22:53.566 align:middle
in the world, the
10-year Treasury.

22:53.896 --> 22:56.796 align:middle
It's the risk-free asset
upon which every price

22:57.096 --> 23:00.746 align:middle
of virtually every asset
in the world is related to.

23:01.356 --> 23:02.266 align:middle
So I'll say three things.

23:02.266 --> 23:04.006 align:middle
First is economic strength.

23:04.006 --> 23:07.866 align:middle
Part of the reason why we've
seen, over the course of 2026,

23:08.276 --> 23:11.446 align:middle
long-term yields go up is,
the economy has strengthened.

23:12.456 --> 23:15.886 align:middle
Second reason, competition
for capital.

23:16.546 --> 23:20.006 align:middle
The surge in capital
expenditures, which I referenced

23:20.006 --> 23:24.496 align:middle
in my remarks, is real, and
the so-called hyperscalers are

23:24.496 --> 23:26.456 align:middle
out in the market
raising funding.

23:26.736 --> 23:28.576 align:middle
And so the competition
for capital is real,

23:28.576 --> 23:31.876 align:middle
and I think it partly explains
the increase in yields.

23:32.446 --> 23:34.506 align:middle
The third is geopolitics.

23:34.586 --> 23:36.766 align:middle
The situation hot spots

23:36.766 --> 23:40.136 align:middle
around the world are
driving long-term yields.

23:40.426 --> 23:45.726 align:middle
It's not simply spot prices of
energy or spot prices for corn

23:45.726 --> 23:49.426 align:middle
or soybeans or wheat,
but it's the difference

23:49.426 --> 23:52.996 align:middle
between those spot prices and
so-called crack spreads-what

23:52.996 --> 23:55.086 align:middle
that means for products
that find their way

23:55.086 --> 23:58.436 align:middle
into stores across the country.

23:58.476 --> 24:00.956 align:middle
I think those are the
three leading explanations

24:00.956 --> 24:02.536 align:middle
but certainly not
an exclusive list.

24:02.536 --> 24:03.466 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

24:03.766 --> 24:04.226 align:middle
Michael McKee.

24:04.406 --> 24:04.986 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

24:05.986 --> 24:08.436 align:middle
Michael McKee from Bloomberg
Radio and Television.

24:09.086 --> 24:12.866 align:middle
You said in Jackson Hole that
you want to see inflation come

24:12.866 --> 24:16.736 align:middle
down clearly and at sufficient
speed, which is a standard

24:16.966 --> 24:19.556 align:middle
without necessarily a
measurable threshold in it.

24:19.556 --> 24:23.986 align:middle
The reason I ask is because
today you say today's policy

24:23.986 --> 24:26.106 align:middle
action will support
a timelier return

24:26.106 --> 24:29.076 align:middle
to the Committee's 2 percent
target, and yet, in the Summary

24:29.076 --> 24:30.166 align:middle
of Economic Projections,

24:30.166 --> 24:33.036 align:middle
the median pushes the 2
percent target achievement

24:33.036 --> 24:35.966 align:middle
out to 2029, another two years.

24:36.266 --> 24:38.976 align:middle
And I'm wondering how you
can square those two things.

24:39.486 --> 24:40.106 align:middle
CHAIRMAN WARSH.

24:40.106 --> 24:41.916 align:middle
One easy way to square
that, Mike,

24:41.916 --> 24:43.676 align:middle
is those aren't my forecasts.

24:44.166 --> 24:48.106 align:middle
Those are the forecasts of my
18 colleagues, and I've tried

24:48.106 --> 24:51.126 align:middle
to represent them
dutifully to you.

24:52.026 --> 24:54.906 align:middle
My business is to not
give forward guidance.

24:55.086 --> 25:00.506 align:middle
But my commitment in June was to
reaffirm to the American people,

25:01.036 --> 25:03.926 align:middle
to anyone listening, that we
will deliver price stability.

25:04.396 --> 25:06.766 align:middle
My commitment in
July was to say,

25:07.186 --> 25:08.936 align:middle
we want to buy a
little bit of time.

25:09.046 --> 25:11.756 align:middle
We want to evaluate what's
happening across a range

25:11.756 --> 25:15.326 align:middle
of dimensions, and what I said
in Jackson Hole in August is,

25:15.326 --> 25:18.116 align:middle
we're committed to a
discipline, not to a decision.

25:18.596 --> 25:23.326 align:middle
Today's action starts to show
we're serious about this.

25:23.696 --> 25:27.006 align:middle
And we will deliver on the
price-stability objective,

25:27.506 --> 25:31.396 align:middle
and as the statement said, we'll
do it on a-on a timelier basis.

25:31.826 --> 25:33.016 align:middle
That's our decision.

25:33.346 --> 25:38.576 align:middle
And when we continue our
discussions over the course

25:38.576 --> 25:40.086 align:middle
of the next several
weeks and months,

25:40.386 --> 25:41.606 align:middle
we'll have more to say about it.

25:41.606 --> 25:44.576 align:middle
But I'm ill-prepared to
prejudge those future actions.

25:44.576 --> 25:45.826 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

25:45.826 --> 25:47.126 align:middle
Matt, from CNN.

25:47.126 --> 25:49.146 align:middle
MATT EGAN.

25:50.136 --> 25:51.026 align:middle
Thanks, Chairman Warsh.

25:51.146 --> 25:52.146 align:middle
Matt Egan with CNN.

25:52.576 --> 25:56.746 align:middle
You've spoken in the past about
the positives that could come

25:56.746 --> 25:59.136 align:middle
from widespread adoption
of artificial intelligence.

25:59.786 --> 26:02.226 align:middle
How concerned are
you, if at all,

26:02.226 --> 26:05.116 align:middle
about these increasingly
alarming warnings we've heard

26:05.116 --> 26:07.916 align:middle
from AI leaders about
losing control

26:07.916 --> 26:11.096 align:middle
of this powerful technology
and doing real-world damage,

26:11.136 --> 26:13.346 align:middle
damage that would presumably
impact the real economy?

26:13.946 --> 26:14.696 align:middle
CHAIRMAN WARSH.

26:14.696 --> 26:17.306 align:middle
So I've spent a lot of
time thinking about AI,

26:17.306 --> 26:20.606 align:middle
and before I found my way
to this post, I spent a lot

26:20.606 --> 26:22.456 align:middle
of time talking about
it publicly.

26:23.846 --> 26:25.576 align:middle
Independence of the
Federal Reserve is

26:25.576 --> 26:26.956 align:middle
about staying in our lane.

26:27.556 --> 26:30.656 align:middle
We care very much
about what's happening

26:30.706 --> 26:32.096 align:middle
in artificial intelligence.

26:32.126 --> 26:34.816 align:middle
We care much about the
implications on the demand side

26:34.816 --> 26:37.896 align:middle
of the economy and ultimately on
the supply side of the economy.

26:38.446 --> 26:39.856 align:middle
I care so much-I think it's

26:39.856 --> 26:43.916 align:middle
so important-that we established
a task force that should report

26:43.916 --> 26:46.716 align:middle
by the end of the year to help
us think about the implications

26:46.716 --> 26:48.386 align:middle
for our future policy
conjuncture.

26:49.026 --> 26:53.816 align:middle
But the policy decisions
that are made about the risks

26:53.816 --> 26:56.256 align:middle
and rewards, the
challenges and opportunities,

26:56.626 --> 26:58.686 align:middle
those are decisions made by
other parts of the government.

26:58.686 --> 27:02.156 align:middle
I'm going to leave it to them to
make those political decisions,

27:02.156 --> 27:03.366 align:middle
those policy decisions.

27:03.746 --> 27:05.826 align:middle
The implications of those
decisions obviously have some

27:05.826 --> 27:08.346 align:middle
bearing on our day job, and
that's where we'll be focused.

27:08.766 --> 27:09.236 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

27:09.286 --> 27:10.246 align:middle
Okay. For the last question,

27:10.246 --> 27:16.116 align:middle
we'll go to Jennifer
Schonberger in the back row.

27:16.576 --> 27:17.306 align:middle
JENNIFER SCHONBERGER.

27:17.626 --> 27:18.536 align:middle
Thank you, Mr. Chairman.

27:18.536 --> 27:20.696 align:middle
Jennifer Schonberger
with Yahoo Finance.

27:20.756 --> 27:23.996 align:middle
Inflation has run up mostly
from higher energy prices

27:23.996 --> 27:26.356 align:middle
and tariffs, which some
say are supply shocks

27:26.396 --> 27:29.516 align:middle
that rate hikes cannot fix
and should fade on their own,

27:29.936 --> 27:32.176 align:middle
so long as inflation
expectations stay anchored.

27:32.546 --> 27:34.336 align:middle
Now that you have hiked rates,

27:34.476 --> 27:37.976 align:middle
do you need to push
growth below potential,

27:38.236 --> 27:40.986 align:middle
unintentionally pushing
weakness on the job market

27:41.236 --> 27:44.576 align:middle
to bring inflation down, and
how do those dynamics play out,

27:44.796 --> 27:45.716 align:middle
given the forcefulness

27:46.006 --> 27:47.956 align:middle
with which AI is driving
the economy right now?

27:48.576 --> 27:48.926 align:middle
CHAIRMAN WARSH.

27:48.926 --> 27:51.376 align:middle
So, there's, there's,
there's a lot there, Jennifer.

27:51.376 --> 27:54.596 align:middle
Let me see if I can't do
just a little bit of it.

27:55.006 --> 28:00.006 align:middle
First, we believe that the
unemployment rate is basically

28:00.006 --> 28:01.996 align:middle
running consistent
with full employment.

28:02.646 --> 28:06.776 align:middle
I don't believe that we need
to do harm to the labor markets

28:06.776 --> 28:08.016 align:middle
to achieve our objective.

28:08.496 --> 28:11.246 align:middle
I don't believe that the
two parts of our mandate,

28:11.816 --> 28:13.476 align:middle
price stability and
full employment,

28:13.756 --> 28:16.586 align:middle
are working at cross
purposes over the medium term.

28:17.096 --> 28:23.336 align:middle
So economic growth-that
is ensuring continuous,

28:23.336 --> 28:27.186 align:middle
sustainable, durable, economic
growth-that's the business

28:27.186 --> 28:27.746 align:middle
we're in.

28:28.046 --> 28:31.106 align:middle
And the job we did today,
the job we'll continue to do,

28:31.506 --> 28:35.146 align:middle
is to ensure price stability,
which can mean that sustainable,

28:35.146 --> 28:37.716 align:middle
durable, economic growth
can go on for longer.

28:38.076 --> 28:41.476 align:middle
The economy can be stronger,
and as I mentioned before,

28:41.476 --> 28:43.576 align:middle
the least well off can
get the benefits of it.

28:44.026 --> 28:44.946 align:middle
JENNIFER SCHONBERGER.

28:45.146 --> 28:45.706 align:middle
Thank you.

28:47.506 --> 28:49.506 align:middle
CHAIRMAN WARSH.

28:50.056 --> 28:51.266 align:middle
Thank you.

